{"title":"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Answer\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Answer</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be a positive integer.\n</p><p>Liu Bang and Xiang Yu have a stick of side length 1 and want to divide it between themselves. Liu marks at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> points on the stick, and then Xiang marks at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> points on the stick. The marked points are distinct. Then, the stick is cut at all marked points, creating a number of pieces. Afterwards, they take turns claiming any unclaimed piece of the stick, with Liu going first. Each player's goal is to maximize the total length of their own pieces.\n</p><p>For each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, determine the largest value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> such that Liu may guarantee a total length of at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />, regardless of Xiang's play.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Answer\">Answer</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Answer\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The largest such value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/3/0f3683d521979451148dff34373ebb4707560b79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^n}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/pfi6DWlXyrI\">https://youtu.be/pfi6DWlXyrI</a> (including motivation discussion, by Dedekind Cuts)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let each of the partitioned sticks be of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/3/ea365f07c0cc08355a508db4c1e064ab18b46322.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_{2n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"110\" height=\"13\" />, in decreasing order, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/1/2216c356f1fd4a7ee45bb2835e9b3c2c3964d103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1 \\geq a_2 \\geq \\dots \\geq a_{2n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"155\" height=\"16\" />. Note that then, Liu always takes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b194663174063e6a60f1d9000cd489897071c293.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_3, \\dots, a_{2n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"127\" height=\"14\" />. Hence, we must find the minimum of \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/3/f0324d068a4dbf87444e48d3c78e68c7cbf22327.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{1 \\leq i = \\text{odd} &lt; 2n} a_i\\]\" width=\"95\" height=\"40\" />.\n</p><p><b>Lemma 1:</b> Liu guarantees maximality when taking sticks in a geometric progression of common ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/8/7a831bee940f52124f66502351d219605f958ab6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2^{n+1} -1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />.\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 1:</b>\n</p><p>Liu wants to partition the sticks such that no matter where or how he cuts the larger stick, he can still obtain a net growth. Suppose he creates a cut of two sticks <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/a/4daec1b65b2c7204eba605d45b19b072005b0218.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A &lt; B$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" />. Now, if Xiang leaves <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> uncut, Liu will grab <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> first. Otherwise, if he cuts <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, the worst possible case is that he cuts it into direct halves. To not render <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> useless, Liu can cut <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> into double that of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, hence it is a net gain greater than or equal to 0. But the thing is, if we have only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> as our leading sticks, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> double that of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, then three new sticks are formed after Xiang cuts, from which by Pigeonhole Principle, Liu obtains the last stick, hence winning the game.\n</p><p>We can guarantee that doubling is the best possible strategy for Liu. If he were to cut triple of quadruple that into pieces <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, Xiang can make some number of cuts with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> cuts such that all pieces are useless in comparison to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. (Indeed, with the max length 1, there is more than enough <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> to pass around, as you can check via the base cases.)\n</p><p>So, via the doubling strategy, each of the sticks have lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/3/1735fe24f41ed85700e90d003db98bfeb8891397.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\delta(1, 2, 4, \\dots, 2^n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"127\" height=\"20\" />. Here, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/4/0a42a8b725c200085e5253db2d2fbf8dab898a8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\delta$\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" /> is our common ratio. This inner sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/d/bedd98c21ffb839a0c08b7c71e6f4f597f58b053.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{n+1}-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"65\" height=\"15\" />. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/3/6536e120157a0646117afe37819687595bc15edb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\delta(2^{n+1} -1) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"124\" height=\"21\" /> hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/7/f075fd719c02521b8f3a7d1d5fa4de0f16bccbd5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\delta = \\frac{1}{2^{n+1} -1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"105\" height=\"40\" />, as desired.\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2:</b> Regardless of Xiang's plays, Liu can guarantee <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/9/ba93b155952bc420a799f4d8bdbaf9cae9d43d87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^n}{2^{n+1} -1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" /> of minimum length.\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 2:</b>\n</p><p>Suppose he were to make these cuts in the common ratio found in <b>Lemma 1</b>. Then, given such <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> cuts, the only plausible moveset that Xiang could play is to split all given segments in half. That is, he MUST split the larger <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> segments in half, whereas the last segment of length 1 is as desired. Then, Liu obtains all odd numbered lengths when ordered <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/2/7c2dcb9bcf9fcebdfa3409cfa0d88e46f04a58d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1 \\rightarrow a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"13\" /> in decreased order, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/6/5a61e12b7739804b65c5a2edf9eb7817800d0459.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\delta(1+1+2+4+\\dots+2^{n-1}) = \\delta(2^n - 1 + 1)= \\delta \\cdot 2^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"421\" height=\"21\" />, which implies the length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/3/0f3683d521979451148dff34373ebb4707560b79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^n}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />, as desired. \n</p><p><b>2026 IMO Q3:</b>\n</p><p>We denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> to be the minimum length regardless of Xiang's plays. If we prove that Xiang plays optimally yet we still obtain a minimum length, then we may guarantee that Liu obtains a length greater than or equal to this minimum by definition. That is, through <b>Lemma 2</b>,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/9/db999a55050a5ca7833633b7574784ca18e7523f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[c = \\boxed{\\frac{2^n}{2^{n+1} - 1}} \\quad \\square\\]\" width=\"142\" height=\"50\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We claim that the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/3/0f3683d521979451148dff34373ebb4707560b79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^n}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />. First, we will show that Xiang can always guarantee a length for themself of at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/0/9d01ea49f3a39d73959acb7132de40553d999176.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^n-1}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />. If Liu does not use all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> marks, then Xiang can simply bisect each segment resulting from Liu's marks, and Xiang will end up with a length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f3cc0ad966b3e637cd199607cbf0d1befc0e491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" />. Say then that Liu does use all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> marks, resulting in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> lengths. We assume that Xiang cuts the stick at all of the points Liu marks before making their own marks.\n</p><p>Consider the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/6/d06ff0c90bc6f81e31faecaba622165272fe2cda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{n+1}$\" width=\"32\" height=\"15\" /> sums of subsets of these lengths. Since each sum is in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e861e10e1c19918756b9c8b7717684593c63aeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" />, two of these sums will have difference at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/6/d860bd5f0dac9c1c2456293de5132769a0764827.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />. Say that these sums correspond to subsets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/f/1bf9ca5e0837dccd49d61a624050446b8f026dd5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$J,K\\subset[n+1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"113\" height=\"20\" />. Xiang may perform the following procedure:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Draw a vertical line in the sand.</li>\n<li>Take all of the pieces in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/8/d984157d061563efda80de103df9cd5800b0543e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$J\\setminus(J\\cap K)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" /> and line them up horizontally end to end, making one continuous segment of pieces, so that the left end of the first stick touches the line.</li>\n<li>Take all of the pieces in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/1/621053b8ae32f42a493febf92065edb9025dd710.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K\\setminus(J\\cap K)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" /> and line them up in the same fashion, just below the first set of sticks.</li>\n<li>At any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-value where one segment has a break and the other segment does not, mark the other segment at that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-value. Note that the number of marks made will be at most one less than the number of the sticks which have been placed in the sand.</li>\n<li>For every piece not placed in the sand (those in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/8/8f816e49efc09d8227f3365dff61dba059a9b3f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[n+1]\\setminus(J\\cup K)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"134\" height=\"20\" />), mark them in their center.</li></ul>\n<p>At the end of this process, there will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> pairs of pieces which are the same length, and one piece which has length at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/6/d860bd5f0dac9c1c2456293de5132769a0764827.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />. We claim that this guarantees a length for Xiang of at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/0/9d01ea49f3a39d73959acb7132de40553d999176.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^n-1}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />. To prove this:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Draw a vertical line in the sand.</li>\n<li>Line up all of the pieces of sticks from largest to smallest, such that for each stick its left end touches the vertical line.</li>\n<li>Below all of the pieces of sticks, add a ruler whose left end aligns with the vertical line.</li>\n<li>As Liu and Xiang take pieces, if Liu's piece is larger than Xiangs, or if Liu is taking the last piece, cover the piece of the ruler with tape from the length of Xiang's piece to the length of Liu's piece.</li></ul>\n<p>Note that in this process, the total length of tape will be at most the length of our un-paired piece, proving our first part of the claim.\n</p><p>Now say that Liu marks the stick so that the resulting pieces would have lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/4/a54abaffa7c05aca767dd6939fedc9d3c3e179d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^k}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"43\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/b/12b1ca2cc74ca3018042eb2fc03b4f23c58dcb24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"113\" height=\"20\" />. We wish to show that Xiang cannot make marks so as to guarantee a length for themself of more than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/0/9d01ea49f3a39d73959acb7132de40553d999176.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^n-1}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" />. We will compute a slightly different value. In particular, it is equivalent to show that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/e/b2eae45b2391e225e33e253b74d2dcf9bf5a2577.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1-(\\text{Liu&#39;s length} - \\text{Xiang&#39;s length})\\]\" width=\"255\" height=\"18\" />\nis at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/8/ca84adea79ca9a952381bf1d201d34ec014b68c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^{n+1}-2}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"42\" />. There is a simple way of calculating this value. Once the stick has been marked by both players and cut, we can say that they take their pieces of the stick simultaneously. When they are each holding their piece, we can take two pieces of tape, each the length of the shorter of the two pieces of sticks being held, and place one piece of tape on each piece of stick. If only Liu has a piece of stick, as there were an odd number of pieces of stick to begin with and this is the last one, we do not place any tape. The total of the lengths of these pieces of tape will be the value we seek. We say that each pair of pieces of tape has its own unique shared color.\n</p><p>Now say that after placing these pieces of tape, we perform the following simple procedure:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Re-arrange the pieces of the stick so that they are where they were before the markings were made, making one continuous segment of pieces.</li>\n<li>At each of Liu's markings, separate the segment of pieces into two segments of pieces.</li></ul>\n<p>Now we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> segments of pieces of sticks, of lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/4/a54abaffa7c05aca767dd6939fedc9d3c3e179d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^k}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"43\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/b/12b1ca2cc74ca3018042eb2fc03b4f23c58dcb24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\in\\{0,\\ldots,n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"113\" height=\"20\" />, upon which there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> pairs of pieces of tape. Take each segment which has two pieces of tape of the same color and set it aside. Say that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> segments remain, noting that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/e/67e6630318d8cb28de2723180b6d359b972489c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />. On them, at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> colors of tape will remain, and for each color, there will be at most two pieces of tape, and if there are two, then they will be on different segments. Consider then how much of the longest remaining segment can be covered in tape. Indeed, no more than the sum of the lengths of the other remaining segments. Thus, at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/6/d860bd5f0dac9c1c2456293de5132769a0764827.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"40\" /> of that segment will not be covered in tape, proving that the total length of tape is at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/c/0ac9f85e80a44961225ccdbf7bbbd0cb629e2d22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^{n+1}}{2^{n+1} - 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"72\" height=\"42\" />. We are done.\n</p><p>~flyingpurplepeopleeater\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO\" title=\"2026 IMO\">2026 IMO</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions,_with_authors\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors\">IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematics_competitions_resources\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Mathematics competitions resources\">Mathematics competitions resources</a></li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO\" title=\"2026 IMO\">2026 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018034858\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.035 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.049 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 414/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 894/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser f